  (1, 'BNT-000101', '9780735211292', 'Atomic Habits', 'James Clear', 'Avery', 'Hardcover', 1, 'Get one percent better every day, and let the maths do the rest.', 'James Clear argues that lasting change does not come from big resolutions but from tiny habits repeated daily. He explains how habits form, why good ones fade and bad ones stick, and offers a practical system for building routines that fit the person you want to become.', '# About this preview

Atomic Habits is still in copyright, so BookNest does not show pages from the book itself. This short preview was written by our librarians to help you decide whether it is for you.

Get one percent better every day, and let the maths do the rest.
---PAGE---
# What it is about

James Clear argues that lasting change does not come from big resolutions but from tiny habits repeated daily. He explains how habits form, why good ones fade and bad ones stick, and offers a practical system for building routines that fit the person you want to become.
---PAGE---
# Three ideas you will meet

Small improvements compound. Getting slightly better each day adds up to a very different year.

Focus on systems, not goals. Winners and losers often share the same goals; what differs is the daily process.

Make good habits obvious, attractive, easy and satisfying, and do the reverse for the habits you want to drop.
---PAGE---
# Is it for you?

For anyone who has set the same resolution twice and wants a method that survives a bad week.

Hardcover, 320 pages. Published by Avery. First published in 2018. ISBN 9780735211292.', 'preview', 39.9, 4.4, 2018, 320, 24, 'covers/bnt-000101.svg', 'Cover of Atomic Habits by James Clear', 1, 1, 'approved', NULL, NOW() - INTERVAL 30 DAY - INTERVAL 37 MINUTE),
  (2, 'BNT-000102', '9780062457714', 'The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F*ck', 'Mark Manson', 'HarperOne', 'Hardcover', 1, 'A blunt, funny argument for caring about fewer things, and caring about them properly.', 'Mark Manson pushes back against relentless positivity. His point is that our attention is limited, problems never disappear, and a good life comes from choosing which struggles are worth it. Sweary, direct and more thoughtful than the title suggests.', '# About this preview

The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F*ck is still in copyright, so BookNest does not show pages from the book itself. This short preview was written by our librarians to help you decide whether it is for you.

A blunt, funny argument for caring about fewer things, and caring about them properly.
---PAGE---
# What it is about

Mark Manson pushes back against relentless positivity. His point is that our attention is limited, problems never disappear, and a good life comes from choosing which struggles are worth it. Sweary, direct and more thoughtful than the title suggests.
---PAGE---
# Three ideas you will meet

You only have so much care to give, so spend it on what matches your values.

Happiness comes from solving problems, not from avoiding them.

Taking responsibility for your reactions is different from taking the blame for what happened.
---PAGE---
# Is it for you?

For readers tired of cheerful self help who want honesty with a sense of humour.

