# What it is about

Computer science professor Cal Newport makes the case that long stretches of undistracted concentration produce the work that matters most. The first half explains why; the second gives rules for protecting focus in a world of notifications, meetings and open plan offices.
---PAGE---
# Three ideas you will meet

Deep work produces results that are hard to copy; shallow work mostly keeps you busy.

Attention is trained like a muscle, and constant switching weakens it.

Schedule focus on purpose: fixed hours, clear rituals and a firm end to the working day.
---PAGE---
# Is it for you?

For students and knowledge workers who end busy days unsure what they actually finished.

