# What it is about

Stanford psychologist Carol Dweck summarises decades of research into two beliefs about ability. People with a fixed mindset see talent as set; people with a growth mindset see it as something built through effort. The difference shapes school, sport, work and relationships.
---PAGE---
# Three ideas you will meet

A fixed mindset treats failure as a verdict; a growth mindset treats it as information.

Praising effort and strategy helps children more than praising intelligence.

Mindsets are not permanent traits. They can be noticed, and changed, in specific situations.
---PAGE---
# Is it for you?

For students, parents, teachers and coaches, and anyone who avoids things they might be bad at.

